---
title: Prettier's style, at native speed
summary: tidy is a code formatter written in Rust. One binary prints what Prettier prints, for JavaScript, TypeScript, HTML, CSS, Markdown, YAML, JSON, TOML and more.
---

{{#var bench = $data.bench}}
{{#var claims = [
  [
    "Matches Prettier",
    "Every release is checked against Prettier " +
      $data.release.prettier +
      " over a 12,000-file corpus: 100% of 3,906 JavaScript files and 3,308 of 3,309 TypeScript files.",
  ],
  [
    "Formats the code inside your code",
    "Styles and scripts in HTML and Vue. Markdown code blocks and front matter. CSS, GraphQL, HTML and Markdown in JS template literals.",
  ],
  [
    "Configurable, if you need it",
    "Reads <code>tidy.json</code>, <code>tidy.jsonc</code> or <code>tidy.toml</code>, on top of <code>.editorconfig</code>. The defaults are Prettier&rsquo;s.",
  ],
  [
    "Stable output",
    "Where Prettier would change its mind on the next run, tidy prints where it settles, so <code>tidy check</code> stays green in CI.",
  ],
]}}
{{#var langs = [
  ["JavaScript, TypeScript, JSX", "Prettier"],
  ["HTML, Vue, Handlebars, MJML", "Prettier"],
  ["CSS, SCSS, Less", "Prettier"],
  ["JSON, JSONC, JSON5", "Prettier"],
  ["YAML, GraphQL, Markdown", "Prettier"],
  ["XML, SVG", "@prettier/plugin-xml"],
  ["TOML", "taplo"],
  ["Dockerfiles", "dprint-plugin-dockerfile"],
  ["INI, .editorconfig, .npmrc", "prettier-plugin-ini"],
]}}
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<div class="wrap rail">
  <main id="main">
    <section class="row hero">
      <p class="num">00</p>
      <div class="body">
        <h1>Prettier's style, at <mark>native speed</mark>.</h1>
        <p class="lede">tidy is a code formatter written in Rust. One binary
          prints what Prettier prints, for every language below. It does not
          shell out to Node &mdash; though there is a Node binding if you want
          one.</p>
        <div class="term">
          <div class="tbar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>~/project</span></div>
          <div class="tin"><em>$</em><code>curl -fsSL https://tidy.lukeed.com |
              sh</code><button
              type="button"
              data-copy="curl -fsSL https://tidy.lukeed.com | sh"
            >copy</button></div>
        </div>
        <p class="alt">or build it with cargo &mdash;
          <a href="/docs/install/">every way to install</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="row sec">
      <p class="num">01 / Speed</p>
      <div class="body">
        <div class="stat"><b>18.5s &rarr; 280ms</b><span>A
            {{ $utils.num(bench.total.files) }}-file corpus of every language,
            checked end to end.</span></div>
        <div class="bars">
          {{#each bench.featured as row}}
            <div class="grp">
              <div class="gl"><b>{{ row.corpus }}</b><span>{{
                    $utils.num(row.files)
                  }}
                  files</span></div>
              {{#each $utils.bars(row) as bar}}
                <div
                  class="b{{#if bar.me}} me{{/if}}{{#if bar.off}} off{{/if}}"
                >
                  <span class="who">{{ bar.who }}</span>
                  <span class="track"><i
                      style="width:{{ bar.width }}%"
                    ></i>{{#if bar.off}}<em>{{ bar.times }}&times; slower</em>{{/if}}</span>
                  <span class="ms">{{ $utils.ms(bar.ms) }}</span>
                </div>
              {{/each}}
            </div>
          {{/each}}
        </div>
        <p class="note">{{ bench.machine }}, all cores, mean of both run orders.
          Bars compare the three native tools; Prettier is off that scale.
          <a href="/docs/benchmarks/">every corpus and the method &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="row sec">
      <p class="num">02 / Output</p>
      <div class="body">
        <h2>Byte for byte what Prettier prints</h2>
        <p class="sub">Not a lookalike style. tidy's tests run Prettier over the
          same corpus and compare the bytes, so a difference is a bug &mdash;
          unless it is one of the
          <a href="/docs/deviations/">documented deviations</a>.</p>
        <div class="ba">
          <div class="pane in"><h3><span>routes.ts</span><span>as written</span></h3>{{{
              $data.sample.input
            }}}</div>
          <div class="pane"><h3><span>routes.ts</span><em>tidy up</em></h3>{{{
              $data.sample.output
            }}}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="row sec">
      <p class="num">03 / Why</p>
      <div class="body">
        <div class="claims">
          {{#each claims as claim}}
            <div><b>{{ claim[0] }}</b><p>{{{ claim[1] }}}</p></div>
          {{/each}}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="row sec">
      <p class="num">04 / Languages</p>
      <div class="body">
        <h2>Every language Prettier formats</h2>
        <p class="sub">Plus the ones its plugins cover, built into the same
          binary. Nothing to install, nothing to register.</p>
        <ul class="langs">
          {{#each langs as lang}}
            <li><b>{{ lang[0] }}</b><span>{{ lang[1] }}</span></li>
          {{/each}}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="row sec">
      <p class="num">05 / Config</p>
      <div class="body">
        <h2>Configurable, if you need it</h2>
        <p class="sub">Each file takes the nearest
          <code>tidy.json</code>,
          <code>tidy.jsonc</code>
          or
          <code>tidy.toml</code>, on top of
          <code>.editorconfig</code>. Shared keys apply everywhere, and a
          language section overrides them. See
          <a href="/docs/options/">every option</a>.</p>
        <div class="cfg">
          <figure class="code"><div class="bar"><span
                class="file"
              >tidy.json</span></div>{{{ $data.sample.config }}}</figure>
          <div>
            <p class="sub" style="margin-top:0">Then run it:</p>
            <figure class="code"><div class="bar"><span>Shell</span></div>{{{
                $data.sample.usage
              }}}</figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</div>

